 - [20261023-190929-bug-the-delay-since-first-attemp](20261023-190929-bug-the-delay-since-first-attemp/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity bug fix whose regression test has to observe state during an attempt (a `statistics` key, a `retry_state` field, a callback argument). The "first fail on unfixed code" run can be masked: an exception raised inside the decorated function is retried, and one raised in a callback is hidden by `suppress(Exception)`. So the red run either passes or fails for some other reason. **then** Put the exception-path facts in `skills/tenacity-change-contract`, the on-demand skill that loads for this kind of change: - which test class owns the area (e.g. `TestStatistics`) - read mid-attempt state from a `before`/`after` callback via `retry_state.retry_object` - callback exceptions propagate raw, while exceptions in the wrapped function get retried - only exhausted retries raise `RetryError`, so suppress only `RetryError` - which loop methods are shared through `BaseRetrying.begin()` and which are re-implemented in `tenacity/asyncio/` Don't rely on red-before-green alone to catch a test that fails for the wrong reason.
 - [20261023-190230-bug-tenacity-after-log-crashes-w](20261023-190230-bug-tenacity-after-log-crashes-w/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity bug fix where the reported failure (an exception or wrong log text) can be reproduced from a unit test built with the existing `tests/test_tenacity.make_retry_state` helper, the gate runs only pytest, and ruff, mypy and PyYAML can't run in the environment. **then** Keep the red-before-green step in `system.md`: write the regression test, run it alone by node id against the unfixed code, and confirm it fails with the reported error before editing `tenacity/`. Don't add a `scripts/` checker that builds a /tmp venv to install lint tools; ensurepip aborts here. Put the "lint/types/YAML can't run, report them as not run" fact in `tool_guidance.md` instead.
 - [20261023-185633-bug-tenacity-utils-find-ordinal](20261023-185633-bug-tenacity-utils-find-ordinal/EXPERIENCE.md) — **when** A tenacity task that changes behaviour a library user can notice (a bug fix, changed log or message text, new API, dropped Python version) where the gate only runs pytest. Repo conventions such as a reno release note in `releasenotes/notes/` are not enforced by CI or the gate, so a harness that only says "make the smallest change and pass the checks" leaves them out. **then** Write unenforced deliverable conventions into the on-demand skill `skills/tenacity-change-contract`, with the exact file path pattern, the YAML section key and the house entry style, and have it load for any user-visible change under `tenacity/`. Don't put them in always-on `system.md`. Keep the skill's lint-tooling advice to the known-working steps (throwaway `/tmp` venv, ruff on changed files only) so discovering the tooling doesn't take over the run.
